@meta
id: 08-02
title: Counts, and the backwards one
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: delete-d
par: 16
minutes: 4
vscode: select the digits with the mouse and press Delete
open: config.ts

@brief
Counts worked on motions in chapter four: `5j` is five lines down. They
work on verbs too, and they mean exactly what you would expect.

Somebody pasted an extra three zeros into the timeout and typed the retry
limit three times over. The file should read `30000` and `5`.

Two ways in. One deletes forwards from where you stand; the other deletes
backwards, which is useful when the easiest thing to land on is the
character *after* the mess.

@keys
| `3x` | delete three characters from the cursor forwards |
| `X` | delete the character before the cursor |
| `2X` | delete the two characters before the cursor |
| `f0` | forward to the next `0` |

@hints
Both edits are a run of characters, not one character. A count in front
of `x` takes the whole run in one press.
--
For the retry limit the easy landing spot is the comma at the end of the
line, and the digits you want gone are behind it. `X` deletes backwards.
--
`f0` lands on the first zero of the timeout, then `3x`. Then `j`, `$` to
the comma, and `2X`.

@solution
    j f0 3x  j $ 2X  :wq<Enter>

Thirteen keystrokes. Two ideas are worth keeping.

First, a count in front of a verb multiplies the verb, and it never has
to be exact — `3x` and `xxx` do the same thing, but `3x` is one decision
instead of three, and one `u` undoes it.

Second, `X` exists because landing on the character *after* something is
often easier than landing on the thing itself. `$` is a free, reliable
anchor. `2X` from there needs no aiming at all. Picking the cheap anchor
and deleting towards the target is a habit that pays off for the rest of
vim.

@start config.ts
export const config = {
  timeoutMs: 30000000,
  retryLimit: 555,
};

@expected config.ts
export const config = {
  timeoutMs: 30000,
  retryLimit: 5,
};
